{"slug":"/students/subjects/chemistry/electrochemistry-redox/discharge-factors/","source":"students/subjects/chemistry/electrochemistry-redox/discharge-factors.html","area":"students","kind":"topic","title":"Factors Affecting Ion Discharge - Chemistry","description":"","subject":"chemistry","body":"<!-- Header -->\n    <nav class=\"bg-white shadow-sm fixed top-0 w-full z-50\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4\">\n            <div class=\"flex justify-between h-16 items-center\">\n                <div class=\"flex items-center\">\n                    <a href=\"/students/subjects/chemistry/electrochemistry-redox/\" class=\"text-rose-600 hover:text-rose-800 mr-4\">\n                        <span class=\"mi -arrow-left\" data-mi=\"arrow_back\">arrow_back</span>\n                    </a>\n                    <h1 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900\">Factors Affecting Ion Discharge</h1>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"flex items-center space-x-4\">\n                    <div class=\"text-sm text-gray-600 hidden sm:block\">\n                        Progress: <span id=\"progressPercent\">0%</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"w-24 sm:w-32 h-2 bg-gray-200 rounded-full\">\n                        <div id=\"progressBar\" class=\"h-2 bg-rose-500 rounded-full transition-all duration-300\" style=\"width: 0%\"></div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"flex items-center gap-3\">\n                        <div class=\"text-sm text-gray-600\">\n                            <span id=\"topic-progress-text\" data-bidemy=\"progress-text\">0% Complete</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class=\"w-24 h-2 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden\" data-bidemy=\"progress\">\n                            <div id=\"topic-progress-bar\" class=\"h-full bg-gradient-to-r from-rose-400 to-pink-500 transition-all duration-300\" style=\"width: 0%\"></div>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </nav>\n\n    <!-- Main Content -->\n    <main class=\"pt-20 pb-8 px-4 max-w-5xl mx-auto\">\n        <!-- Learning Objectives -->\n        <div class=\"bg-rose-50 border-l-4 border-rose-500 p-4 rounded-r-lg mb-6 animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-rose-800 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -bullseye mr-2\" data-mi=\"adjust\">adjust</span>Learning Objectives</h3>\n            <ul class=\"text-rose-700 text-sm space-y-1\">\n                <li>• Explain the factors that determine which ion is discharged at an electrode</li>\n                <li>• Apply the electrochemical series to predict discharge products</li>\n                <li>• Understand the effect of concentration on selective discharge</li>\n                <li>• Explain overpotential and its effects</li>\n                <li>• Predict products of electrolysis for different electrolytes</li>\n            </ul>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 1: Introduction -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">1</span>\n                Introduction to Selective Discharge\n            </h2>\n            \n            <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">During electrolysis, when an electrolyte contains more than one type of cation or anion, <strong>which ion gets discharged?</strong> The answer depends on several factors working together.</p>\n            \n            <div class=\"bg-amber-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-amber-500 mb-4\">\n                <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-amber-700 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -question-circle mr-2\" data-mi=\"help\">help</span>The Key Question</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\">If an electrolyte contains multiple ions (e.g., CuSO₄ solution has Cu²⁺, H⁺, SO₄²⁻, OH⁻), which ions will be discharged at each electrode?</p>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4\">\n                <div class=\"bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-blue-700 mb-2 text-center\"><span class=\"mi -minus-circle mr-2\" data-mi=\"help_outline\">help_outline</span>At the Cathode (Reduction)</h4>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\">Cations (positive ions) gain electrons</p>\n                    <p class=\"text-center mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-blue-800\">Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻ → M</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-red-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-red-700 mb-2 text-center\"><span class=\"mi -plus-circle mr-2\" data-mi=\"add_circle\">add_circle</span>At the Anode (Oxidation)</h4>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\">Anions (negative ions) lose electrons</p>\n                    <p class=\"text-center mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-red-800\">Aⁿ⁻ → A + ne⁻</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 2: The Electrochemical Series -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">2</span>\n                Factor 1: Position in the Electrochemical Series\n            </h2>\n\n            <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">The <strong>electrochemical series</strong> ranks ions by their tendency to be discharged. Ions lower in the series are discharged preferentially.</p>\n\n            <div class=\"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4\">\n                <div class=\"bg-purple-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-purple-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-purple-700 mb-3 text-center\"><span class=\"mi -arrow-down mr-2\" data-mi=\"arrow_downward\">arrow_downward</span>Cations (At Cathode)</h4>\n                    <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-500 text-center mb-2\">Listed from LEAST to MOST easily discharged</p>\n                    <div class=\"space-y-1 text-sm\">\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\">\n                            <span class=\"font-medium\">K⁺</span>\n                            <span class=\"text-xs text-gray-400\">least easily discharged</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Na⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Ca²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Mg²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Al³⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Zn²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Fe²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Ni²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Sn²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Pb²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-yellow-100 p-2 rounded border border-yellow-400\">\n                            <span class=\"font-medium text-yellow-700\">H⁺</span>\n                            <span class=\"text-xs text-yellow-600\">hydrogen</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Cu²⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Ag⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">Au³⁺</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-green-100 p-2 rounded border border-green-400\">\n                            <span class=\"font-medium text-green-700\">Cu⁺, Ag⁺, Au⁺</span>\n                            <span class=\"text-xs text-green-600\">most easily discharged</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"bg-teal-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-teal-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-teal-700 mb-3 text-center\"><span class=\"mi -arrow-up mr-2\" data-mi=\"arrow_upward\">arrow_upward</span>Anions (At Anode)</h4>\n                    <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-500 text-center mb-2\">Listed from MOST to LEAST easily discharged</p>\n                    <div class=\"space-y-1 text-sm\">\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-green-100 p-2 rounded border border-green-400\">\n                            <span class=\"font-medium text-green-700\">Halides: I⁻ &gt; Br⁻ &gt; Cl⁻</span>\n                            <span class=\"text-xs text-green-600\">most easily discharged</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\">\n                            <span class=\"font-medium\">OH⁻</span>\n                            <span class=\"text-xs text-gray-400\">hydroxide</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">NO₃⁻</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">SO₄²⁻</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">CO₃²⁻</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-white p-2 rounded\"><span class=\"font-medium\">PO₄³⁻</span></div>\n                        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center bg-yellow-100 p-2 rounded border border-yellow-400\">\n                            <span class=\"font-medium text-yellow-700\">F⁻</span>\n                            <span class=\"text-xs text-yellow-600\">least easily discharged</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-green-500\">\n                <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-green-700 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -lightbulb mr-2\" data-mi=\"lightbulb\">lightbulb</span>Key Rule</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\"><strong>At the cathode:</strong> Cations below H⁺ in the series (Cu²⁺, Ag⁺, Au³⁺) are discharged instead of H⁺. If the cation is above H⁺, then H⁺ from water is discharged instead.</p>\n                <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mt-2\"><strong>At the anode:</strong> Halide ions are discharged before OH⁻. If no halide is present, OH⁻ from water is discharged.</p>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 3: Effect of Concentration -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">3</span>\n                Factor 2: Concentration of Ions\n            </h2>\n\n            <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">The <strong>concentration</strong> of ions can override the electrochemical series predictions, especially for chloride ions.</p>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-yellow-500 mb-4\">\n                <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-yellow-700 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -flask mr-2\" data-mi=\"science\">science</span>Concentration Effect on Chloride</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mb-2\"><strong>Low concentration Cl⁻:</strong> OH⁻ is discharged (oxygen produced)</p>\n                <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\"><strong>High concentration Cl⁻:</strong> Cl⁻ is discharged (chlorine produced)</p>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4\">\n                <div class=\"bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-blue-700 mb-2\">Dilute NaCl Solution</h4>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mb-2\"><strong>Cathode:</strong> H⁺ discharged → H₂ gas (H⁺ from water)</p>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mb-2\"><strong>Anode:</strong> OH⁻ discharged → O₂ gas</p>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\"><strong>Overall:</strong> Na⁺ and Cl⁻ remain in solution</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"bg-orange-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-orange-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-orange-700 mb-2\">Concentrated NaCl Solution</h4>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mb-2\"><strong>Cathode:</strong> H⁺ discharged → H₂ gas</p>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm mb-2\"><strong>Anode:</strong> Cl⁻ discharged → Cl₂ gas</p>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\"><strong>Overall:</strong> NaOH remains in solution</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-red-500 mt-4\">\n                <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-red-700 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -exclamation-triangle mr-2\" data-mi=\"warning_amber\">warning_amber</span>Important Note</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\">The concentration effect is most significant for chloride. For very dilute solutions, OH⁻ is discharged. For concentrated chloride solutions, Cl⁻ is discharged. This is the basis of the <strong>chlor-alkali industry</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 4: Nature of Electrodes -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">4</span>\n                Factor 3: Nature of Electrodes\n            </h2>\n\n            <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">The electrode material affects which products are formed. <strong>Inert electrodes</strong> (Pt, C/Graphite) do not participate in reactions. <strong>Reactive electrodes</strong> can be oxidized.</p>\n\n            <div class=\"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4\">\n                <div class=\"bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-gray-700 mb-3 text-center\"><span class=\"mi -circle mr-2\" data-mi=\"circle\">circle</span>Inert Electrodes</h4>\n                    <ul class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm space-y-1\">\n                        <li>• <strong>Platinum (Pt)</strong></li>\n                        <li>• <strong>Graphite (Carbon)</strong></li>\n                        <li>• Do not react during electrolysis</li>\n                        <li>• Only transfer electrons</li>\n                        <li>• Products depend on ions present</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"bg-orange-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-orange-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-orange-700 mb-3 text-center\"><span class=\"mi -cog mr-2\" data-mi=\"settings\">settings</span>Reactive (Active) Electrodes</h4>\n                    <ul class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm space-y-1\">\n                        <li>• <strong>Copper, Silver, etc.</strong></li>\n                        <li>• Anode can dissolve</li>\n                        <li>• Cathode can gain metal coating</li>\n                        <li>• Used in electroplating</li>\n                        <li>• Anode reaction: M → Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-green-500\">\n                <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-green-700 mb-2\"><span class=\"mi -industry mr-2\" data-mi=\"help_outline\">help_outline</span>Electroplating with Copper</h4>\n                <ul class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm space-y-1\">\n                    <li>• <strong>Anode:</strong> Pure copper → dissolves (Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻)</li>\n                    <li>• <strong>Cathode:</strong> Object to be plated → copper deposits (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu)</li>\n                    <li>• <strong>Electrolyte:</strong> CuSO₄ solution</li>\n                    <li>• The concentration of Cu²⁺ in solution remains constant</li>\n                </ul>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 5: Overpotential -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">5</span>\n                Factor 4: Overpotential\n            </h2>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-purple-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-purple-500 mb-4\">\n                <p class=\"text-purple-800 font-medium\"><strong>Overpotential</strong> is the extra voltage needed beyond the theoretical value to discharge an ion at an electrode. It depends on the electrode material and the ion being discharged.</p>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4\">\n                <div class=\"bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-blue-700 mb-2\">Effects of Overpotential</h4>\n                    <ul class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm space-y-2\">\n                        <li>• O₂ and H₂ have high overpotential on many electrodes</li>\n                        <li>• This can affect which ion is discharged</li>\n                        <li>• Platinum has low overpotential for H₂</li>\n                        <li>• Mercury has high overpotential for H₂</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"bg-amber-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-200\">\n                    <h4 class=\"font-semibold text-amber-700 mb-2\">Practical Example</h4>\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 text-sm\">When electrolysing concentrated NaCl with a mercury cathode, Na⁺ is discharged instead of H⁺ because H₂ has very high overpotential on mercury. This produces sodium amalgam (Na/Hg) used industrially.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 6: Summary Table -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">6</span>\n                Summary: Predicting Products\n            </h2>\n\n            <div class=\"overflow-x-auto\">\n                <table class=\"w-full text-sm border-collapse\">\n                    <thead>\n                        <tr class=\"bg-rose-50\">\n                            <th class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 text-left\">Electrolyte</th>\n                            <th class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 text-left\">Cathode Product</th>\n                            <th class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 text-left\">Anode Product</th>\n                            <th class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 text-left\">Explanation</th>\n                        </tr>\n                    </thead>\n                    <tbody>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 font-medium\">CuSO₄ (aq)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Copper (Cu)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Oxygen (O₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Cu²⁺ below H⁺; SO₄²⁻ above OH⁻</td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 font-medium\">AgNO₃ (aq)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Silver (Ag)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Oxygen (O₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Ag⁺ below H⁺; NO₃⁻ above OH⁻</td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 font-medium\">NaCl (dilute)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Hydrogen (H₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Oxygen (O₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Na⁺ above H⁺; dilute Cl⁻</td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 font-medium\">NaCl (conc.)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Hydrogen (H₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Chlorine (Cl₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Na⁺ above H⁺; concentrated Cl⁻</td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 font-medium\">CuCl₂ (aq)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Copper (Cu)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Chlorine (Cl₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Cu²⁺ below H⁺; Cl⁻ is halide</td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr class=\"bg-gray-50\">\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3 font-medium\">PbBr₂ (molten)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Lead (Pb)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Bromine (Br₂)</td>\n                            <td class=\"border border-gray-200 p-3\">Only ions present</td>\n                        </tr>\n                    </tbody>\n                </table>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 7: Video -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mr-3 text-sm font-bold\">7</span>\n                Video: Selective Discharge Explained\n            </h2>\n            \n            <div class=\"bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border\">\n                <div class=\"video-container\">\n                    <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/dLVDVn7ZX5M\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Section 8: Practice Problems -->\n        <div class=\"section-card animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h2 class=\"text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-4 flex items-center\">\n                <span class=\"mi -pencil-alt text-rose-600 mr-3\" data-mi=\"edit\">edit</span>\n                Practice Questions\n            </h2>\n\n            <div class=\"space-y-4\">\n                <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n                    <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">1. Predict the products of electrolysis of dilute CuSO₄ solution using inert electrodes.</p>\n                    <details class=\"mt-2\">\n                        <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Answer</summary>\n                        <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700\">\n                            <strong>Cathode:</strong> Copper (Cu) - Cu²⁺ is below H⁺ in the electrochemical series<br>\n                            <strong>Anode:</strong> Oxygen (O₂) - SO₄²⁻ is above OH⁻ in the series<br><br>\n                            <strong>Equations:</strong><br>\n                            Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu<br>\n                            Anode: 4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n                    <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">2. Explain why chlorine is produced at the anode when concentrated NaCl is electrolysed, but oxygen is produced when dilute NaCl is used.</p>\n                    <details class=\"mt-2\">\n                        <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Answer</summary>\n                        <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700\">\n                            <strong>Concentrated NaCl:</strong> The high concentration of Cl⁻ ions makes them discharge preferentially, producing Cl₂ gas. The concentration effect overrides the fact that OH⁻ should be discharged based on the electrochemical series.<br><br>\n                            <strong>Dilute NaCl:</strong> The low concentration of Cl⁻ means OH⁻ ions (from water) are discharged instead, producing O₂ gas. Without high concentration, the electrochemical series order is followed.\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n                    <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">3. What happens at the electrodes during copper purification by electrolysis?</p>\n                    <details class=\"mt-2\">\n                        <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Answer</summary>\n                        <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700\">\n                            <strong>Anode (Impure copper):</strong> Copper dissolves<br>\n                            Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻<br><br>\n                            <strong>Cathode (Pure copper strip):</strong> Copper deposits<br>\n                            Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu<br><br>\n                            <strong>Result:</strong> Impurities fall to the bottom as \"anode sludge\" and pure copper is deposited on the cathode.\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-yellow-200\">\n                    <p class=\"font-medium text-gray-800 mb-2\">4. Predict the products of electrolysis of molten PbBr₂ and aqueous PbBr₂.</p>\n                    <details class=\"mt-2\">\n                        <summary class=\"text-sm text-blue-600 cursor-pointer hover:text-blue-800\">Show Answer</summary>\n                        <div class=\"mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700\">\n                            <strong>Molten PbBr₂:</strong><br>\n                            • Cathode: Lead (Pb) - only Pb²⁺ present<br>\n                            • Anode: Bromine (Br₂) - only Br⁻ present<br><br>\n                            <strong>Aqueous PbBr₂:</strong><br>\n                            • Cathode: Lead (Pb) - Pb²⁺ below H⁺<br>\n                            • Anode: Bromine (Br₂) - Br⁻ is a halide, discharged before OH⁻\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Key Takeaways -->\n        <div class=\"bg-gradient-to-r from-rose-50 to-pink-50 p-5 rounded-lg border border-rose-200 animate-fade-in-up\">\n            <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-rose-800 mb-3\"><span class=\"mi -star mr-2\" data-mi=\"star\">star</span>Key Takeaways</h3>\n            <ul class=\"text-rose-700 text-sm space-y-2\">\n                <li>• Electrochemical series determines discharge order when concentration is similar</li>\n                <li>• At cathode: Cations below H⁺ are discharged instead of H⁺</li>\n                <li>• At anode: Halides are discharged before OH⁻; high [Cl⁻] overrides this</li>\n                <li>• Inert electrodes (Pt, C) don't react; active electrodes participate in reactions</li>\n                <li>• Overpotential affects discharge of gases, especially H₂ and O₂</li>\n            </ul>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Complete Topic Button -->\n        <div class=\"mt-8 pt-6 border-t border-gray-200\">\n            <div class=\"flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-4\">\n                <div class=\"text-center sm:text-left\">\n                    <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-gray-800\">Ready to Complete?</h3>\n                    <p class=\"text-sm text-gray-600\">View all sections, then take the quiz to mark this topic as complete.</p>\n                </div>\n                <button id=\"topic-complete-btn\" disabled=\"\" class=\"px-6 py-3 rounded-xl bg-gray-300 text-gray-500 cursor-not-allowed opacity-50 flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-medium\">\n                    <span class=\"mi -lock mr-2\" data-mi=\"lock\">lock</span>\n                    <span>Complete (0% viewed)</span>\n                </button>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <!-- Navigation -->\n        <div class=\"flex justify-between items-center mt-8\">\n            <a href=\"/students/subjects/chemistry/electrochemistry-redox/electrolysis-basics/\" class=\"px-4 py-2 text-gray-600 hover:text-gray-900\">\n                <span class=\"mi -chevron-left mr-2\" data-mi=\"chevron_left\">chevron_left</span>Previous\n            </a>\n            <a href=\"/students/subjects/chemistry/electrochemistry-redox/electrolysis-calculations/\" class=\"px-4 py-2 bg-rose-500 text-white rounded-lg hover:bg-rose-600\">\n                Next: Electrolysis Calculations<span class=\"mi -chevron-right ml-2\" data-mi=\"chevron_right\">chevron_right</span>\n            </a>\n        </div>\n    </main>\n\n    \n\n<!-- Topic Tracking and Quiz System -->\n  \n\n\n<div data-bidemy=\"quiz\"></div>","bodyClass":"bg-gray-50 min-h-screen","bodyStyle":"","css":["@keyframes fade-in-up {\n            from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(20px); }\n            to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }\n        }\n        .animate-fade-in-up { animation: fade-in-up 0.5s ease-out forwards; }\n        .section-card {\n            background: white;\n            border-radius: 12px;\n            padding: 1.5rem;\n            margin-bottom: 1.5rem;\n            box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);\n            border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;\n        }\n        .video-container {\n            position: relative;\n            width: 100%;\n            padding-bottom: 56.25%;\n            height: 0;\n            overflow: hidden;\n            border-radius: 8px;\n            background: #f9fafb;\n        }\n        .video-container iframe {\n            position: absolute;\n            top: 0; left: 0;\n            width: 100%;\n            height: 100%;\n            border: none;\n        }"],"quiz":{"topicId":"discharge-factors","subject":"chemistry","passingScore":60,"questions":[{"question":"Which ion is discharged preferentially at the cathode: Cu²⁺ or H⁺?","options":["H⁺ because hydrogen is more reactive","Cu²⁺ because copper is below hydrogen in the electrochemical series","Neither - both are discharged equally","It depends on the concentration only"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Cu²⁺ is discharged preferentially because copper is below hydrogen in the electrochemical series. Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu"},{"question":"At the anode, which ion is discharged first: Cl⁻ or OH⁻ (in concentrated solution)?","options":["OH⁻ is always discharged first","Cl⁻ is discharged first because it's a halide","Both are discharged simultaneously","Neither - other anions are discharged"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Halide ions (Cl⁻, Br⁻, I⁻) are discharged before OH⁻. In concentrated solution, Cl⁻ is oxidized to Cl₂."},{"question":"What is the effect of concentration on the discharge of chloride ions?","options":["Concentration has no effect","High concentration favors Cl⁻ discharge; low concentration favors OH⁻ discharge","Low concentration favors Cl⁻ discharge","Concentration only affects the rate, not the product"],"correct":1,"explanation":"At high Cl⁻ concentration, chloride is discharged (producing Cl₂). At low concentration, OH⁻ from water is discharged (producing O₂)."},{"question":"Why does sodium NOT deposit at the cathode during electrolysis of NaCl(aq)?","options":["Sodium is too heavy","Sodium ions are too small","Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so H⁺ is discharged instead","Sodium reacts immediately with water"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Na is above H in the electrochemical series (more reactive), so H⁺ from water is discharged preferentially: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂"},{"question":"What is overpotential?","options":["The voltage produced by the cell","Extra voltage needed beyond the theoretical value to discharge an ion","The potential difference between electrodes","The maximum voltage a cell can produce"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Overpotential is the additional voltage required beyond the theoretical value to drive an electrode reaction at a practical rate."},{"question":"What products are formed at the electrodes when CuSO₄(aq) is electrolyzed with inert electrodes?","options":["Cathode: H₂, Anode: O₂","Cathode: Cu, Anode: O₂","Cathode: Cu, Anode: Cl₂","Cathode: H₂, Anode: SO₂"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Cu²⁺ is below H⁺, so Cu deposits at cathode. SO₄²⁻ is not discharged (above OH⁻), so OH⁻ is oxidized to O₂ at anode."},{"question":"In electroplating with copper, what happens at the anode?","options":["Copper deposits on the anode","The anode dissolves: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻","Oxygen gas is evolved","The anode remains unchanged"],"correct":1,"explanation":"In electroplating, the copper anode dissolves (Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻), maintaining Cu²⁺ concentration in solution."},{"question":"Which statement about inert electrodes is correct?","options":["They participate in the reaction","They are made of reactive metals like sodium","They only transfer electrons without participating in reactions","They are always the cathode"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Inert electrodes (platinum, graphite) only transfer electrons and do not participate in the electrode reactions."}]},"tracker":{"topicId":"discharge-factors","topicTitle":"Factors Affecting Ion Discharge","subject":"chemistry","sections":["section-1","section-2","section-3","section-4","section-5","section-6","section-7","section-8"],"quizRequired":true},"links":[]}